      "claim": "Nalmefene, a related N-containing bicyclic opioid, has an absolute oral bioavailability of 41% in humans, supporting F ≥ 20% for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C28"],
      "claim": "Pentazocine has a low absolute oral bioavailability of 18%, but its phenolic hydroxyl and prenyl substituents create first-pass metabolic liability absent in the query, so this contradictory evidence is outweighed.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high: multiple high-transfer human bioavailability measurements for N-methylpiperidine and spiro-piperidine analogues (meperidine 48–63%, fenspiride ~90%, nalmefene 41%) support F ≥ 20%; the only low-F analogue, pentazocine (18%), is structurally divergent due to phenol/prenyl groups and does not override the supportive pattern.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct human pharmacokinetic measurement exists for the exact query molecule.",
    "First-pass metabolism and transporter efflux for the spiro oxetane/piperidine scaffold are not experimentally characterized."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

